  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nThis lesson explores how web apps respond to user actions and introduces how JavaScript, events, and the DOM enable that interactivity. Through hands-on comparisons of frustrating and effective designs, students connect interactivity to user-centered design. They examine how interaction choices shape user experience and can involve trade-offs.",
        "preparation": "- Provide copies of the AI and Algorithmic Unit Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide copies of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide (one per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nExplore how interactions affect user experience and consider how design choices can improve or complicate how a web app works."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we use JavaScript to make our\nwebsites more interactive?**\n\nStudents connect the role of a front-end developer to building interactivity with JavaScript, then build a mental model of event-driven interactions by acting out how user actions are detected and trigger responses. They use the Select → Listen → Respond pattern to make buttons interactive and ensure their code correctly connects to their HTML, using AI as a tool to support and check their work.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we use JavaScript to make our\nwebsites more interactive?**\n\nAct out how clicks trigger changes on a page, then use the Select → Listen → Respond pattern to make buttons interactive and check that the code connects correctly to the page, using AI as a tool to help along the way."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how programs track and update changing values using variables, operations, and state. Through unplugged simulation, guided code exploration, and AI-supported code generation, they connect abstract programming concepts to real-world interactions. The lesson emphasizes evaluating AI-generated code, testing behavior, and maintaining consistency between stored data and what appears on screen.",
        "preparation": "- Prepare blank sheets of paper for students to use in the changing values simulation activity\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore how apps keep track of changing information using variables and updates. You act out how values change, examine how code makes those changes happen, and use AI to generate, test, and explain how data connects to what you see on the screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nIn this lesson, students refactor repeated event handler code into named arrow functions to make behavior reusable and easier to understand. They compare different ways of organizing code and connect this to abstraction as a way to simplify and clarify logic. Students also use AI as a support tool to generate, test, and refine functions while evaluating whether the code matches intended behavior.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nIn this lesson, you make your code cleaner and easier to reuse by turning repeated behavior into named functions and organizing how your app works. You test and refine your functions, using AI as a helper while making sure the code matches what you intended."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nStudents connect flowchart-based decision making to real app behavior by translating visual logic into JavaScript if/else statements within an interactive app. They use AI as a coding partner to generate, revise, and debug conditional logic, focusing on how decisions in code change what users see and experience. The lesson emphasizes aligning variables, conditions, and outputs across flowcharts and code.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nTurn flowchart decisions into JavaScript code that controls how an app responds to user actions. Work with AI to build and debug conditional logic that changes what users see on the screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nIn this lesson, students extend their work with functions by introducing parameters for flexibility and reuse. They will use their pattern recognition skills to identify reusable processes and possible parameters, moving from flowchart to code. Students author and test their own parameterized function, using AI for hints and debugging while remaining accountable for understanding and verifying any suggested code.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the Functions with Parameters activity guide if your students will not use Sketch Lab",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nYou will learn how to make a function more flexible and reusable using parameters—- one or more variables that are passed into a function. This knowledge will allow you to convert many specialized functions into more general functions that can make your code more efficient and organized. You will use AI for hints and debugging while remaining accountable for understanding and verifying any suggested code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate how dynamic web pages respond to user interactions by analyzing the relationship between events, state, logic, and updates. They examine interactive features using POUR and explain how code controls what changes on the page. Students plan and generate a dynamic feature using variables, conditionals, and functions, and evaluate how effectively it supports usability and accessibility.",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore how web pages respond to user actions by connecting events, data, and logic to what changes on the screen. You build a dynamic feature and evaluate how well it works for users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nStudents deepen their understanding of conditional logic by creating programs that use nested if-else statements  to model multi-step decisions. They trace and design multi-step flowcharts, translate them into JavaScript, and systematically test all possible outcomes to ensure their logic behaves as intended.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Nested Conditionals activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nLearn how to use nested if-else statements to handle situations where one decision depends on another. Plan a flowchart, turn it into code, and test all possible outcomes to make sure the conditional logic works correctly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nStudents build on their knowledge of conditional logic by learning AND and OR operators. They learn how to fill out a truth table for all possible outcomes and diagram out a flowchart with multiple outcomes. They turn their flowcharts into code and test out all possible True/False combinations to make more robust web pages.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide (one for each student)\n- (Optional) Print copies of the unplugged Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nLearn how to chain together multiple conditionals using AND and OR operators to account for a larger range of user actions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nThis lesson focuses on understanding and evaluating complex conditional logic using the NOT operator and other logical operators. Students analyze existing code, use AI to explain and critique logic, and refactor nested conditionals into combined Boolean expressions. The lesson also emphasizes evaluating how rules impact different users, including considerations of fairness and inclusivity.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nUse the NOT operator to understand and improve how conditional rules work. Analyze and refactor existing code, then evaluate how those rules affect different users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nIn this 5-day project, students design and build an interactive web app that reacts to user input using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Through milestones that move from planning and algorithmic logic to coding, testing, and reflection, students practice computational thinking and iterative design.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Reactive Logic Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nIn this project, you’ll create your own interactive web app that responds to user actions in real time. You’ll plan your app’s logic, build and test its code using JavaScript, and refine it through feedback and reflection. By the end, you’ll have a finished app that shows how your ideas, logic, and creativity work together to make something dynamic and fun."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
