  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "cc4be6f5-cbac-4770-8dcd-254a40f546f2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc4be6f5-cbac-4770-8dcd-254a40f546f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cfe99ff7-5f41-4171-8309-d29d4bbea69c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6842e9e-4d0d-48af-bd66-8abe060c70b9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students distinguish between actions (things happening) and elements (parts of a webpage) as a foundation for understanding interactivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** \nGive each group a copy of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide.\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6842e9e-4d0d-48af-bd66-8abe060c70b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3c39ba8-1c61-40b4-835f-39c15749c4d9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students silently sort the items into two groups: actions (things happening) and parts of the page. Have students compare with a partner before sharing with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that all the items identified as actions are called **events**, and all the parts of the page are **elements**. Connect this back to what they just sorted so it feels like naming their ideas, not introducing new content."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3c39ba8-1c61-40b4-835f-39c15749c4d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cba0f95d-47fc-4c36-a6f7-f70fb02f12ac",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one action you wish your project from Unit 1 could have done?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that their previous projects included parts of a webpage but did not respond to user actions. They begin to distinguish between an action (event) and what the page does in response, setting up the need for interactivity.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may describe outcomes (\"show a message\") instead of events (\"click a button\"). If needed, guide them to name the action first, then what the page should do in response."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cba0f95d-47fc-4c36-a6f7-f70fb02f12ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c21e1f3-5520-46ba-a34c-ee4d38dc2b56",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c21e1f3-5520-46ba-a34c-ee4d38dc2b56",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "946dddbb-33a5-4958-830e-77551fe21a19",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Last unit you designed web pages, think about the pages you built. Your pages had elements, but could they respond to events or do any of the things we just listed? In unit 1, you focused on designing what a page looks like. In this unit, you learn how to make your pages interactive.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "946dddbb-33a5-4958-830e-77551fe21a19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f6f494d-e009-4569-ab01-406f1cc9cab7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f6f494d-e009-4569-ab01-406f1cc9cab7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59914f58-4321-466a-8af6-59a611210d63",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the DOM is what allows parts of the page to be changed\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nShow the annotated webpage and point out specific parts such as the button, text, image, and input. Remind students that these are the parts they identified earlier in the warm-up. Briefly point to the diagram on the right to show that these parts can be grouped and organized within the page, but do not go into detail about the structure. Explain that when something happens on a page, like a click or typing, that is an **event**, and events happen to these parts. Emphasize that when events happen, these parts of the page can change.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "DOM and JavaScript Introduction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the structure diagram and assume they need to understand how everything is organized. Keep the focus on the idea that a webpage is made of parts that can change. Treat the diagram as a quick visual reference, not something students need to analyze in detail."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59914f58-4321-466a-8af6-59a611210d63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1f12ce6-a91c-4270-a1eb-38580d4f8f32",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nDefine DOM. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide. Emphasize: \"parts of a webpage\" and \"can change.\" Briefly note that something (code) has to tell the page what to change, and that they will look at what causes those changes next.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the term \"object\" or get confused by technical language. Keep the explanation grounded in what they can see: \"The DOM is what lets the page change\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1f12ce6-a91c-4270-a1eb-38580d4f8f32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c9c8b32-d664-4f00-8c39-3d07301e94c8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n Briefly explain that when something changes on a webpage, it does not happen automatically—there is code that tells the page what to do. That code is called JavaScript. Explain that JavaScript listens for user actions (events) and tells the page how to respond. Let students know this is the language they will use in this unit to make their web apps interactive.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the user action itself (\"I clicked\") causes the change. Reinforce that something in the page is \"watching\" for that action and telling the page what to do. Keep the explanation simple; focus on the idea that JavaScript is what makes the page respond, not how the code works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c9c8b32-d664-4f00-8c39-3d07301e94c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56a82d80-3052-4e93-9e1b-a554f004259d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n Walk through the diagram step-by-step, pointing as you go. Ask students what they notice changed, then focus on the middle steps to explain how the change happens; emphasizing that JavaScript is the middle step, connecting the user action to what changes on the page. Explain:\nF- irst, the page is loaded\n- Then the user does something, like clicking a button\n- JavaScript is watching for that action and detects it when it happens\n- JavaScript tells the page what to change \n- That change happens in the DOM (the parts of the page) \n- Finally, the browser shows the updated page. \n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56a82d80-3052-4e93-9e1b-a554f004259d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99a96a18-a656-46b2-90fd-855b11b59392",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1. Explain that the page has many parts, but it does not respond to events yet. Students explore the page and identify its parts in their activity guide, then decide which parts could change. Students choose one interaction to make work and use AI Tutor to make that change happen. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** \n Look for students who can identify multiple parts of the page and clearly describe what they want to change. Ask: \"What part are you focusing on\" or \"What do you want the page to do?\" or \"What happens right now when you select a button?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Static vs Interactive Web App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may try to make multiple parts work at once. Encourage them to focus on one interaction and get it working first. If students are stuck, prompt them to describe what the user does and what should change on the page before using AI.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99a96a18-a656-46b2-90fd-855b11b59392",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3488fbd1-af45-408c-8824-61343b7e305d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  *What made the page change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students may say \"the button,\" \"clicking,\" or \"the AI.\" Guide students to recognize that the page changed because code made it respond. Introduce that this code is JavaScript, which allows the page to update. Emphasize that these updates happen in the DOM, which is what allows parts of the page to change.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the action (\"I clicked\") or the tool (\"the AI\") instead of the underlying cause. Acknowledge their responses, then help them connect the idea that code made the page respond. Avoid going into detail about how the code works—focus on the idea that JavaScript enables the change."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3488fbd1-af45-408c-8824-61343b7e305d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bca6bb06-4cdd-4e0f-bb8f-316a32ce326f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that interactivity affects user experience and that some interactions work better than others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that interactivity means a page responds when a user does something, like clicking, typing, or other events. Keep the explanation short and grounded in examples students have already experienced.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Trade-Offs in Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bca6bb06-4cdd-4e0f-bb8f-316a32ce326f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64961871-d19d-4df1-8ebe-218730cff8b6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just made a page interactive. But here's the next question… does every interaction automatically make a page better? Let's test that. You're going to try two versions of an app that both have interaction and compare how easy they are to use.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume that \"more interaction = better.\" Do not correct this immediately. Let students test this idea in the next activity so they can experience the difference themselves.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64961871-d19d-4df1-8ebe-218730cff8b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1bee3a0-a8e2-41ad-8ce6-853801f34e47",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- **Level 2:** Students experience an app that has a lot of frustrating interactivity.\n- **Level 3:** Students experience an app of the same purpose that has a much better user experience. \n\nEmphasize that their goal is to compare the two experiences and think about how the design affects the user.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are able to describe what is frustrating in Level 2 and what works better in Level 3. Ask: \"What made this version harder to use\", \"What made the other version easier?\", or \"What changed for the user?\"",
        "progression_name": "Document Design Flaws",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on surface features (colors, layout) instead of interaction. Guide them to focus on what happens when they click, type, or try to complete a task. Encourage students to complete the task in each app rather than just clicking around so they fully experience the frustration or improvement.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1bee3a0-a8e2-41ad-8ce6-853801f34e47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df7a5650-e6e6-430c-92e0-2badaae9de20",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version was better and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that not all interactions improve the user experience and begin to connect interaction to design. Guide students to understand that interactivity is not just about making things happen, but about making things happen in a way that helps the user.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Interactivity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may give general answers like \"this one was better\" without explaining why. Prompt them to describe specific interactions (what happened when they clicked or typed) and how those interactions affected their experience. If students focus only on visual appearance, redirect them to how the app responded to their actions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df7a5650-e6e6-430c-92e0-2badaae9de20",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bcda0b35-4d0a-4d76-b5ed-e09e491e0156",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the last section of the activity guide: Quick Design Sprint. Ensure they choose a specific user to focus on. Students can either draw the app and label their improvements or write a short description of how they would improve it for that user. If students are unsure who to choose, provide examples such as someone using it quickly, someone who gets confused easily, or someone using it on a small screen.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may suggest changes that are not connected to a specific user (e.g., \"make it better\" or \"add more features\"). Prompt them to explain who their change is helping and why. If needed, ask: \"How does this help your user?\" or \"What problem are you solving for them?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bcda0b35-4d0a-4d76-b5ed-e09e491e0156",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5eceb46-f101-4125-9d2e-a356e61f23a8",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Would your change make anything harder for someone else?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that improving an app for one user can sometimes create challenges for others. Guide students to notice that design decisions often involve trade-offs, where making something better in one way may make it harder in another.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume their change is only beneficial and have difficulty identifying downsides. Prompt them to think about different users by asking, \"Who might find this harder to use?\" or \"What could be confusing about this change?\" If needed, provide examples to model how one improvement can create a new challenge.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5eceb46-f101-4125-9d2e-a356e61f23a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59287f99-92c1-45f3-bdb2-9db954ab5cd6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web app responds to a user by describing an event and what changes on the page. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59287f99-92c1-45f3-bdb2-9db954ab5cd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1006605d-ea06-4cbf-a929-dc19cedd3edd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb6721b5-62c7-40f0-aff0-8f87217d9b47",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the role of a front-end developer to building interactivity on a webpage and recognize JavaScript as the tool that makes interactions happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly spotlight the role of a Front-End Developer. Explain that this role is responsible for what users see and how a webpage responds when they interact with it. ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name a website or app where something responds instantly (e.g., a button click, animation, or form). Ask, \"What do you think the developer had to decide to make that interaction work?\" This helps students connect the role to real interactions they use every day.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb6721b5-62c7-40f0-aff0-8f87217d9b47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6926a3a9-caee-4159-93e0-4fe3ce767a91",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** A front-end developer uses JavaScript to make a button respond to a click. If that developer used AI to build a “Like” button, what would they need to tell the AI to make it work?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a developer must clearly describe both the user action and what should happen in response when using AI to build an interaction. They begin to notice that something must detect the action and trigger the response, rather than the change happening automatically. This sets up the idea that interactions follow a structured process, which will be explored through event listeners and event handlers.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6926a3a9-caee-4159-93e0-4fe3ce767a91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb6f205b-7c5f-42f3-97df-91cfee62d473",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb6f205b-7c5f-42f3-97df-91cfee62d473",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75b25e72-03f8-4dd0-9f8b-30ba12583a18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand how webpages respond to user actions by connecting the roles of event listener (detect) and event handler (respond).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define event listener. Emphasize that it is attached to an element and waits for a specific user action (click, hover, key press).",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intro to Event Handling"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75b25e72-03f8-4dd0-9f8b-30ba12583a18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "019dbd2b-a0b0-4bff-98fe-e2a7f3ca741c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define event handler. Emphasize that this is the response — what actually happens after the event.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly form small groups of three to four and assign one role per person: Element, User, Event Listener, and Event Handler. Emphasize that each role represents a part of what happens behind the scenes when someone interacts with a webpage. Give students about 30–60 seconds to decide who will take on each role, and prompt them to read their role name aloud within their group so everyone is clear. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups assign roles, circulate to ensure each group has all roles covered and to clarify confusion, especially between the Event Listener and Event Handler. If needed, briefly restate that the listener waits for the action and the handler decides what happens next.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "019dbd2b-a0b0-4bff-98fe-e2a7f3ca741c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59154e69-72ed-4af7-b98a-f5b77f48d022",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group decide on a simple interaction such as a tap, clap, or press. Once they've chosen their interaction, direct them to say the three planning statements out loud together within their group: what their event is, what the listener is waiting for, and what the handler will do. Emphasize that this is a quick call-and-response style step and should take no more than 1–2 minutes. The goal is for every group to clearly state their plan before acting, not to write anything down or overthink it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups plan, circulate to ensure each group has selected a clear interaction and is actually saying their responses out loud. If a group is quiet or vague, prompt them to say their plan together and be more specific about what will happen.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage groups to keep interactions simple (tap, clap, press) so the focus stays on the sequence, not the scenario.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59154e69-72ed-4af7-b98a-f5b77f48d022",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a6f62e5-e57d-4fcd-97a8-6be628ba9e5f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct them to act it out using the four-step sequence on the slide: the user performs the action, the listener announces that the event was detected and calls \"Run,\" and the handler performs the response. Emphasize that the handler cannot act until the listener calls it, reinforcing the idea that something must detect the event before code runs. Give groups a minute or two to run through the sequence at least once, and encourage them to repeat it with a different response if they finish quickly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students act out the sequence, circulate to ensure groups are following the correct order and using the language of \"event,\" \"listener,\" and \"handler\" appropriately. If a group skips a step or has the handler acting too early, pause them and ask which step might be missing. Pay close attention to confusion between the listener and handler roles, and reinforce that the listener detects and triggers while the handler performs the response.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure groups explicitly say \"Run\" before the handler acts. This step represents the moment code executes and is critical for understanding how event handling works.\n\nIf students confuse the listener and handler roles, restate that the listener detects and triggers, while the handler performs the action.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a6f62e5-e57d-4fcd-97a8-6be628ba9e5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e06cdad1-b483-485b-8536-59c8c8bbc04d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Who detected the event?*\n- *Who triggered the action?*\n- *Who performed the action?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that different parts of the system have distinct roles in making an interaction happen. The event listener is responsible for detecting when a user action occurs, but it does not perform the action itself—it triggers the next step. The event handler is what actually runs and produces the visible change on the page. Through this discussion, guide students to understand the sequence: a user action (event) is detected by a listener, which then calls the handler to perform the response. This reinforces that interactions do not happen automatically—there is a structured process that connects user input to changes on the screen, which will later map directly to how event listeners and event handlers work in code.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e06cdad1-b483-485b-8536-59c8c8bbc04d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29704b5b-3c44-491c-87ca-8b28ce2120e7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the Interacts → Listen → Respond model to how event handling works in a webpage and recognize that event listeners detect actions and trigger event handlers to update the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain that what students just acted out is how event handling works on a webpage:\n- The first step is interacts. The user interacts with an element, like clicking a button. Nothing changes yet — this is just the action the user takes.\n- The second step is listen. The page listens for that action. This is the event listener — it waits for a specific event, like a click, and detects when it happens.\n- The final step is respond. The event listener calls the event handler, and the event handler runs the code. This is where something actually changes on the page, like text updating or an image appearing.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "How to Handle An Event"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29704b5b-3c44-491c-87ca-8b28ce2120e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "928d3a9d-8ea6-4d70-81f5-b76340fc875a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to show that this code represents the same three-step process students just acted out for how a webpage responds to a user action. Have students record the example syntax in their Unit Guide:\n- Step one is select an element. This line of code targets the element on the page (like the button).\n- Step two is add an event listener. This tells the page what event to listen for (like a click).\n- Step three is respond to the event. This is the event handler — the code that runs and updates the page (text, image, color, etc.).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "928d3a9d-8ea6-4d70-81f5-b76340fc875a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99b16f03-a266-4e9a-bc72-412f49eda6dc",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI-generated code may look correct but still not work, and model how to question and verify whether the code actually matches the event or selected element from the prompt. (especially IDs).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99b16f03-a266-4e9a-bc72-412f49eda6dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7ade6fb-89bb-485f-a0a7-386dbc972b82",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI sounds confident and can generate code that looks correct, but it doesn’t actually know if it will work on your specific page. It is making a best guess based on patterns, so when we see something like this ID ‘buttonID,’ we have to stop and ask: does an element with this ID actually exist in our web page, because if it doesn’t, nothing will happen even though the code looks right.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7ade6fb-89bb-485f-a0a7-386dbc972b82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71b35e6c-fda4-4546-b6ef-1a788ca7fe39",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce each debugging step students can use when their button isn't responding. As each tip appears, connect it to how it helps improve their prompt:\n- First, show the element. Emphasize that including a screenshot helps AI understand what part of the page the student is working with.\n- Next, describe the element in detail. Highlight that students should include specific details like text, color, or position so AI can correctly identify it.\n- Finally, confirm the element. Model asking AI to verify which element it’s selecting to make sure it’s targeting the correct one.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71b35e6c-fda4-4546-b6ef-1a788ca7fe39",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1892ec4-4592-43eb-8519-7ad3ced3eca0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When your button isn't working, don’t just guess — be specific. First, show the element so AI can see what you’re working with. Then describe the element in detail — what it says, what it looks like, where it is. And if it’s still not working, ask AI to confirm which element it’s selecting, because sometimes it’s targeting the wrong one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1892ec4-4592-43eb-8519-7ad3ced3eca0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c8ca49d-a10a-46fa-88ad-0e8d810b5a5d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will apply the Select → Listen → Respond model to build interactive features, use AI to generate event-based code, and verify that their code correctly targets elements and updates the page.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Make a Button Respond"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c8ca49d-a10a-46fa-88ad-0e8d810b5a5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d5c54c3-4512-4950-afe0-5ea914a4bbd3",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to apply what you learned by acting like a front-end developer—your job is to make a webpage respond to user actions by selecting elements, adding event listeners, and writing event handlers. You can use AI to help generate code, but you also need to test it and make sure it actually works when you interact with the page.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d5c54c3-4512-4950-afe0-5ea914a4bbd3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af985f57-83f1-4ff8-9750-1313b58d1e40",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- **Level 1:** Predict and trace what the code will do when a button is clicked\n- **Level 2:** Use AI to generate code for one button interaction\n- **Level 3:** Write code yourself to add a second button interaction\n- **Level 4:** Build a full interactive app based on a chosen scenario\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, focus on helping students connect the code to the event handling model. Check that they can identify where the code selects an element, listens for an event, and responds. Ask questions like, “What event is this listening for?” and “What actually changes on the page when you click?” In Levels 2 and 3, support students in working with AI-generated code. Watch for common issues like nothing happening on click or the wrong element responding. Prompt students with, “Which element is this code targeting?” and “How can you tell if it’s the right one?” In Level 4, push students toward fully working interactions. Encourage them to test each button and verify that every interaction works as expected.\n",
        "progression_name": "Adding Interactivity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to test frequently after adding each interaction. Remind them that if nothing happens, it’s usually because the code is not targeting the correct element or the interaction isn’t set up properly.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 1-4 to assess whether students can add an event handler to make a button interactive and update page content when a button is clicked. Look for evidence that students can correctly select elements, attach event listeners, and create responses that change text or visuals on the page."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af985f57-83f1-4ff8-9750-1313b58d1e40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff662afa-887f-4a26-a1e4-9ad3fdee4654",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web page responds to a click by describing both what the user does and what changes on the page. Prompt students to be specific about what should happen when a button is clicked. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review Key Vocabulary"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff662afa-887f-4a26-a1e4-9ad3fdee4654",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e717c33a-741f-4880-b54c-8c83c651f2e6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46b89875-3532-4fdd-94d6-90f7d71407bd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you’re a front-end developer at YouTube, and your job is to decide what videos show up on the homepage. All you can see are the buttons people click and information they submit. Based on just those actions, you have to decide which videos to show millions of people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46b89875-3532-4fdd-94d6-90f7d71407bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a37a775-a8dd-4551-8068-3735fd2fbec6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts. \n- What do you notice when users click, type, or post?\n- What changes each time someone interacts?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the types of data YouTube tracks from user interactions (likes, views, comments, watch time, searches) and recognize that these values update over time. They should also identify the specific parts of the page (like/dislike buttons, play/pause controls, comment fields, search bars) that capture user actions and trigger those updates. Guide students to connect what they see on the screen to underlying variables that are stored and updated behind the scenes.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a37a775-a8dd-4551-8068-3735fd2fbec6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9604cd45-7a52-481f-943c-c76dfde27bbd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What data is YouTube tracking when users interact with the site, and what parts of the page collect that data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the types of data YouTube might track from user interactions (likes, views, comments, watch time, searches) and understand that these values are stored as variables that continuously update over time and shape what users see on the platform. Help students bring in examples (scores, timers, progress bars) from other sites and guide them to connect what they see on the screen to underlying variables that are updated behind the scenes.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9604cd45-7a52-481f-943c-c76dfde27bbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1894e3dc-02cf-46ec-a25b-a5e904e6a4e7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1894e3dc-02cf-46ec-a25b-a5e904e6a4e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "114abd51-a151-400f-ab4b-212b2f9378a6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience how a value can change repeatedly while still being tracked — modeling the concept of a variable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute blank pieces of paper for each group to use for the changing values simulation. ",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Changing Values Simulation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "114abd51-a151-400f-ab4b-212b2f9378a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75f30643-c9bd-4051-9c98-d15ad52d114b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s act out how something keeps track of changes over time. You’ll see how the number changes again and again — but we’ll always know the most recent one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75f30643-c9bd-4051-9c98-d15ad52d114b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83b66214-baad-4b7e-aa9d-3fee95f3b40a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated example to model how the simulation works. Point out how one student writes the starting value, and others take turns giving new values to add or subtract.Then have students begin the simulation:\n- One student writes score = 0 on their paper\n- The “+” and “–” students take turns giving new numbers\n- The score student crosses out the old value and writes the new one each time\n\nEncourage students to say the new score aloud so everyone can follow the changes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they act out the changing values. Check that the score student is correctly updating the number and not keeping old values. Ask:\n- “How do you know what the current score is right now?”\n- “What happens to the old number once a new one is written?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, pause midway and ask: What pattern are you noticing as the number keeps changing? This helps students connect the activity to the idea of something that stores and updates a value over time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83b66214-baad-4b7e-aa9d-3fee95f3b40a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2423942f-6845-4554-b702-ba502d78c912",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** How does keeping track of a value help a web app respond to user actions?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that programs need to keep track of values in order to respond to changes. They should begin to connect this idea to how tracking values makes web apps update and feel more dynamic.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2423942f-6845-4554-b702-ba502d78c912",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c05af74-e31e-4b88-a5b6-ee98292d2e5b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define variable. Connect the definition back to the simulation they just did by explaining that the paper held a number that kept changing — like a score that went up and down. In programming, we call that paper a variable because it’s a container that holds one value at a time. That value can change as the program runs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck on the idea of a variable as a container, help them make the connection by explaining how you can take out what’s inside and replace it with something new but you can only store one thing at a time. For now, students don’t have to know the difference between let (a variable whose value can change) and const (a variable whose value is fixed) but they should recognize that those keywords indicate creating a new variable.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c05af74-e31e-4b88-a5b6-ee98292d2e5b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7963799d-1714-40f4-b4a9-d892d4a1b96c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define state for students and connect it back to the simulation and the variable definition by explaining how our variable’s number kept changing — but at any given moment, it only showed one value. That current value is called the state. It describes what’s happening right now in the program.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can compare this to a snapshot of a game or app at one moment in time. For example: “Right now my score is 200, I have 2 lives left, and the level is 3. That’s the program’s current state.”  This helps students see how state reflects a real-time picture of what the program knows at that exact moment.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7963799d-1714-40f4-b4a9-d892d4a1b96c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ccb8cf8d-03a6-49cf-8fe2-8f537499d880",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define operation. Connect this concept to the earlier simulation by explaining that when someone added or subtracted a number, that was an operation. In code, operations are what make variables change. For example, adding one to your score when you click a ‘like’ button.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students notice that “score” appears twice, explain that’s how the program knows which value to update. The variable’s name on both sides of the equals sign connects the old value to the new one."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ccb8cf8d-03a6-49cf-8fe2-8f537499d880",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45c935a5-a822-43d5-8128-e3ec458aa405",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students state the goal out loud (“It should say Hello Sonia”), then compare it to what AI actually produced. Emphasize the habit: always check if AI gave you what you intended, not just whether it wrote code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students predict the output before running it (“Is this 15 or 510?”), then test it. Emphasize the habit: always run AI code and check the result. Don’t just assume it the code works because it looks right.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students predict what happens step-by-step (“What is `num` after each line?”), then run it. Emphasize the habit: always make sure you can explain what is happening in your code. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45c935a5-a822-43d5-8128-e3ec458aa405",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2c3960d-a997-446f-9012-ecd02f0314f4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 2–5. Explain that they will build the same app by alternating between using AI to generate code and writing their own code to update state.\n- **Level 1:** Students explore a gaming dashboard in Sketch Lab and annotate the variables.\n- **Level 2:** Students explore how variables change by making changes to the dashboard in Web Lab.\n- **Level 3:** Students choose one of the levels and use AI to debug code that should change a variable.\n- **Level 4:** Students reflect on the code they produced.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students explaining what value they are tracking and how it changes. Ask:\n- “What are you tracking?”\n- “What caused it to change?”\n- “What is the value now?”\n- “Does the result match what you expected?”\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Working with Variables",
        "progression_name": "Working with Variables",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students accept AI-generated code quickly, pause them to explain how the value changes before moving on. Emphasize that they must verify that the value updates correctly on the screen."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 to check whether students can:\n- define what the app should do before using AI\n- update a variable correctly\n- explain how the value (state) changes over time\n- compare expected vs actual behavior\n- use AI to generate, test, and verify solutions\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2c3960d-a997-446f-9012-ecd02f0314f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4aaf943b-29fc-4618-9a16-d427cf5a7041",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s put some names to what we just acted out. In code, that paper’s number is like a variable, and the current value it shows is called its state. Let’s see what that looks like in code.",
        "progression_name": "Explore How Values Change",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4aaf943b-29fc-4618-9a16-d427cf5a7041",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7985bfce-c47b-40e4-af98-ced287cb475e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to display each step of how a program stores, changes, and displays a value.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the slide and briefly explain that let is used for values that can change, while const is used for values that stay the same.\n",
        "progression_name": "Debugging Choice Levels"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7985bfce-c47b-40e4-af98-ced287cb475e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "971c319b-2f64-4460-a195-fcff98d41756",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts. \n- *How did AI help you find or fix bugs?*\n- *When did you have to step in to make your own fixes?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that while AI can be a powerful debugging partner, their own reasoning, testing, and understanding of the code are essential for verifying solutions. Guide students to connect debugging with verifying that the program’s state (variable values) and what appears on screen are consistent. Encourage students to mention the any debugging checks they may have practiced:\n- **Name check:** Did AI spell the variable name right and use it consistently?\n- **Value check:** Does the variable have the value I expect? (Try `console.log()` to confirm.)\n- **DOM check:** Is the code changing the correct element or class on the page?\n- **Order check:** Does the code that updates the page run *after* the variable’s value changes?\n\nThese checks help students reason about how and when the program updates information. Also connect this back to AI debugging: AI can help spot likely errors because it recognizes patterns, but students still need to verify the logic — especially when the pattern doesn’t quite fit.\n",
        "progression_name": "Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students bring up cases where the AI’s suggestion didn’t fully solve the problem, highlight that moment as valuable: it shows they’re taking ownership of testing and verifying — a professional debugging mindset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "971c319b-2f64-4460-a195-fcff98d41756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3766d96-c5e4-4aa9-80f4-20e8d5214579",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students give specific examples from their debugged code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3766d96-c5e4-4aa9-80f4-20e8d5214579",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6140da2-7b34-4300-a4e1-edcbbfa1512a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dea56aaf-fe11-4141-9695-a3b9f436fdab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** \nStudents recognize problems caused by repeated code and begin to identify qualities of clean, maintainable code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** \nClick through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What problems might happen if code is repeated many times?*\n- *What would make code easier to work with?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** \n Students should recognize that repeating code can lead to bugs, make updates harder, and slow developers down. They should begin to identify that code is easier to work with when it is organized, readable, and reusable."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dea56aaf-fe11-4141-9695-a3b9f436fdab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f929a9d-f606-4b0a-8496-a41b9e1804cf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \nExplain why clean, organized code matters in real-world development.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f929a9d-f606-4b0a-8496-a41b9e1804cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f0b3c97-8106-4f6c-85a2-30b873e32e41",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f0b3c97-8106-4f6c-85a2-30b873e32e41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fda4b5fd-9f8b-40a8-859c-1a3255a48433",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** \n Students understand that code can be grouped into a single behavior and that functions are used to name and reuse that behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** \n Display the event handler and pause so students can read it. Animate in the purple boxes one by one to highlight the curly braces and the code block inside them. Explain that this entire block works together as one behavior for the button click.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introduction to Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fda4b5fd-9f8b-40a8-859c-1a3255a48433",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81c50d17-0761-48a6-83a0-11b96290b931",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the comparison activity. Direct students to the Unit Guide where they will see two versions of similar code: one using an event handler and one using a named function using arrow notation. Have them:\nlabel each part of both programs\nhighlight what is the same\ncircle what is different\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students annotate, ensure they are identifying where the behavior lives in each program. Watch for students focusing only on syntax differences – redirect them to compare how the behavior is grouped. Clarify that both programs perform the same behavior, but one keeps it inside the event handler while the other moves it into a named, reusable function.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81c50d17-0761-48a6-83a0-11b96290b931",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6c73d9c-5d7d-45eb-ab65-be1162b57b0f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *What is the same?*\n- *What is different?*\n- *Where did the behavior move?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the same behavior is highlighted in both programs, while the structure around it changes. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6c73d9c-5d7d-45eb-ab65-be1162b57b0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3cab6cd3-5f88-4d22-9a18-d12faa735cde",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to reveal the expected annotations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define function. Emphasize that a function is a way to name and reuse behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that const handleClick is used to create and name the function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that () means the function does not take any input.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Clarify that => is the syntax used to define the function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show that {} contains the code that will run when the function is used.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the logic inside {} and explain that it updates the text to \"Button clicked\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that there are two common ways to write arrow functions: named and anonymous. For named arrow functions, emphasize that because it has a name, it can be reused or called later in the code. For anonymous arrow function, clarify that they are often used “in place,” such as inside an event handler or passed into another function."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3cab6cd3-5f88-4d22-9a18-d12faa735cde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "835c6fef-0f36-4f87-ab7f-29b95254bdd5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about it like this, if someone is anonymous, you can not call their name to get their attention. But if you know someone’s name, you can call them anytime you need them. Named functions work the same way – we can call them again later.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "835c6fef-0f36-4f87-ab7f-29b95254bdd5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a957a8b9-5271-45c3-90e9-b549cec10e5b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to apply what you learned by acting like a developer—your job is to take behavior that is currently written inside an event handler and refactor it into a reusable function.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a957a8b9-5271-45c3-90e9-b549cec10e5b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b9b1649-773a-48b1-b311-12bf3c66ad80",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 1 through Level 4.\n- **Level 1:** Predict and trace how the code behaves\n- **Level 2:** Use AI to refactor an event listener to use a function\n- **Level 3:** Refactor an event listener without AI assistance\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, focus on helping students identify where the behavior is located. Check that they can distinguish between code that runs inside the event handler and behavior that could be moved into a function. Ask questions like: “Where is the behavior right now?” “What is this code doing when it runs?” In Levels 2 and 3, support students as they refactor code into functions. Watch for confusion between defining a function and calling it. Prompt students with: “Did you move the behavior into a function?”",
        "progression_name": "Predict & Trace",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to test frequently after making changes. If something doesn’t work, have them check:\n- Is the function defined correctly?\n- Is it being called?\n- Does the name match what it does?\n\nRemind students that AI may generate code that looks correct but still needs to be tested and verified.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 1-3 to assess whether students can meet the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can write arrow functions to organize code into reusable pieces and refactor code into a function instead of keeping behavior inline.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b9b1649-773a-48b1-b311-12bf3c66ad80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a076f460-0d72-4074-b9a4-8fa448035785",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that abstraction hides details and that using named functions helps them organize and reuse behavior when wiring up interactions in an app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define abstraction. Emphasize that abstraction means hiding the details and focusing on what something does. Use the visual to compare what’s happening inside versus using the function name, and highlight how the name helps us understand the behavior without seeing the code.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Abstraction & Writing Reusable Functions ",
        "progression_name": "Refactor with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a076f460-0d72-4074-b9a4-8fa448035785",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ee428e3-b599-4ae3-b431-307608225651",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which function is easier to understand without reading the code? Which one would AI be more confident about?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear function names improve understanding for both humans and AI, while vague names make it harder to predict what the code does.",
        "progression_name": "Write it Yourself"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ee428e3-b599-4ae3-b431-307608225651",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc0e09f6-b66a-466e-ad4b-e6884b76c53e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to act like a developer building an interactive app. You’ll look at an example, then use AI to help you generate functions for new buttons, connect them, and test that they work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc0e09f6-b66a-466e-ad4b-e6884b76c53e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Now you're going to act like a developer building an interactive app. You’ll look at an example, then use AI to help you generate functions for new buttons, connect them, and test that they work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ensure they are following the spec → add named arrow function → add event listener → test process. Check that students are:\n- using the button description in prompt.md\n- generating a named arrow function\n- connecting it using addEventListener\n- testing each button before moving on\n\nOnce they move to code, check:\n- “What is this button supposed to do?”\n- “Does your function match that behavior?”\n- “What changes on the page when this runs?”\n- “Where is your function connected to the button?”\n",
        "progression_name": "Write Reusable Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that AI is generating code based on their description. Clearer descriptions lead to better results. Encourage them to revise their prompt or ask AI to explain the code if something doesn’t work.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 to assess whether students can meet the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Write arrow functions to organize code into reusable pieces\n- Refactor behavior into functions instead of writing it directly inside event handlers\n- Explain how abstraction makes code easier to maintain, including how naming and reusing functions improves clarity\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss their responses to the Question of the Day with a partner. After a few minutes, ask for volunteers to share with the class. Encourage students to use examples from their code today — such as how they renamed a function, created a reusable arrow function, or refactored repeated code — to explain how they made their code cleaner and easier to reuse.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give general answers (like “I used functions”), prompt them to be more specific by asking follow-up questions such as:\n- “What change made your code easier for someone else to understand?”\n- “How did naming or reusing a function help simplify your program?”\n- “How might AI tools benefit from code that’s clearly named and organized?”\n\nThis reinforces both human and AI readability while helping students articulate what clean and reusable really mean in practice.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What functionality of TikTok does this flowchart represent?* \n- *Where else have you seen an app make a decision like this?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps make decisions based on user input or app data, similar to the flowchart. Examples include social media apps deciding which videos to suggest next or login screens checking if username and password match. This helps students recognize that apps are full of decision-making points and anchors abstract conditionals in contexts they know. It also helps them understand decision points shape the user experience based on the result of the condition.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e7d07585-293c-4cf2-be18-3ab6ccdb5f1e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1dee407a-f37a-4976-a5b1-996e23cd5a83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the different flowchart components.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define flowchart.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intro to Flowcharts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1dee407a-f37a-4976-a5b1-996e23cd5a83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f830ade-ae59-4a77-808a-1b8b2d7b23ec",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that flowcharts are a way to visually represent how decisions are made. Using standard symbols means that anyone (or any AI agent) reading the flowchart can understand the logic.\n\nWalk through the following symbols as students review them in their Unit Guide:\n- Start/End → where the process begins or stops\n- Process → an action the app or user takes\n- Decision → a question with Yes/No outcomes\n- Connector → shows the flow of logic\n- Variable → information the app stores\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Direct students to complete the section under the vocabulary. If you want students to complete all vocabulary terms now, jump ahead to the Conditional Statement and Boolean Expression slides. "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f830ade-ae59-4a77-808a-1b8b2d7b23ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a7c572e-bbbd-4e44-8990-2f9ff9adea53",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI uses computer vision to interpret flowcharts. \n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a7c572e-bbbd-4e44-8990-2f9ff9adea53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5014f883-a4b4-4a26-a909-87692c1116e3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Standardization matters not just for humans, but also because it makes it easier for AI to correctly understand and build working programs from a picture of a flowchart.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5014f883-a4b4-4a26-a909-87692c1116e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67e033f6-dd09-4ae3-80e8-1a06b648a83f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the Create a Conditional Flowchart section in their Unit Guide. If students are having trouble getting started, tell them to use the diagram on the slide as a template and fill it in with their plain text. Then click through the animations on the slide to show students an example of how to fill out the chart. Make sure to connect the code snippet to each part of the diagram.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that test cases help test that the flowchart logic works. Make sure students try at least one value for each path of the if/else (e.g., one above and one below the temperature cutoff) and by tracing through the flowchart. If they are having trouble identifying that they need to set the temperature variable to different values, ask them what their condition or decision (yellow diamond) is checking."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67e033f6-dd09-4ae3-80e8-1a06b648a83f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3e73332-624b-4e7c-b144-ad0b2067879f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use flowcharts to generate conditional logic and verify that their code correctly interprets user decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Explore a thermometer app, then write code to change the conditional logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students can identify the main parts of the program: the variable, the event listener, and the if / else condition. Prompt with questions like: “What value is the program checking?” and “What happens when the slider is moved?” Support students in connecting the flowchart steps to the code.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Coding Conditionals with Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "Explore: Temperature App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This level is based on the flowchart that students completed in their Unit Guide. Direct students to trace through the flowchart if they are struggling to understand the code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3e73332-624b-4e7c-b144-ad0b2067879f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e394487-e68b-4719-a6d5-78197799a140",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\nWhat does the condition check for?\nWhat did you change in the conditional logic?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that the condition is checking the value of the temperature variable which is controlled by the slider (or can be hard-coded by the programmer). Students should have made changes to the message text, message color, and background color. They may also have modified the condition, e.g. changed it to temperature > 80."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e394487-e68b-4719-a6d5-78197799a140",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94639b57-eceb-4322-bd71-f8d2fc60a563",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define conditional statement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define Boolean Expression.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94639b57-eceb-4322-bd71-f8d2fc60a563",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2022b4dc-fa54-409e-8442-26d09f326ad5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students practice modeling decisions as flowcharts and translate flowchart decisions into JavaScript if/else statements using AI as a coding partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 and 3:\n- **Level 2:** Students use Sketch Lab to build their own flowchart.\n- **Level 3:** Students use AI to support them in generating code for conditional logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are including all key components including a start, process, decision, and outcome. Each flowchart must incorporate at least one variable and at least one function. Help students who are not sure of how to flowchart by asking prompting questions like: \"What decisions does the app have to make?\" or \"What should the user see on screen?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Creating Conditionals with Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "Turning Flowcharts into Code",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are feeling confused by the flowchart elements, have them start with the yellow diamond decision point. Turn their decision into a question with (at least) two outcomes. Then have them work backwards to figure out any functions and variables needed for making that decision. Encourage students who finish early to incorporate more than one condition into their flowchart.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students understand flowcharting conditional statements. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Able to use diamond shapes to represent a decision point\n- Flowcharting least two outcomes as a result of a decision point (e.g. Yes/No)\n- Able to incorporate at least one variable and at least one function.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2022b4dc-fa54-409e-8442-26d09f326ad5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6cf9c60-c658-4706-b3f0-4dcb14e3853a",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Consider an if-else conditional you included in your code. How does your conditional connect to what the user sees or experiences?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that conditionals can change what is visible on screen, as well as controlling system behavior behind the scenes. This includes things like sound, data, or app state. Students should connect the decision being made in their code to both the user’s experience (what they hear or see on screen) and the underlying changes happening in the program (like volume level or number of items in a cart).",
        "progression_name": "Turn Your Flowchart into Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6cf9c60-c658-4706-b3f0-4dcb14e3853a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8c6a7c8-2910-4cdf-84d3-126e3c286117",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8c6a7c8-2910-4cdf-84d3-126e3c286117",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "56e37027-246f-4d02-accd-569687dc455d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts. \nWhen a customer clicks \"Add to Cart\" for an item of clothing, what information does the website need to know?\nWhat if a piece of information, like size, is missing?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the key pieces of information (e.g., size, color, quantity) needed for the system to complete the action and explain that missing information prevents the process from working correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a84b66f1-e827-441c-bc56-5bb92ec0ac28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will identify the key pieces of information needed to determine an outcome and recognize that the same process can be reused with different inputs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work with a partner to play the pizza guessing game. One student selects a pizza, and the other asks YES/NO questions to identify it. Students should switch roles after each round.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Guess the Pizza",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the game moving by limiting the number of questions or encouraging students to choose questions that narrow down options quickly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e4bb90d-584b-4328-bbd2-4448301ca278",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e4bb90d-584b-4328-bbd2-4448301ca278",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ba8c0f1-f8a8-4869-b16b-f4f3934f28ac",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- Which questions helped you narrow down the options fastest?\n- How could you reuse those same questions to find a different pizza?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify which questions most effectively narrow down the options and recognize that the same set of questions can be reused with different answers to produce different outcomes.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ba8c0f1-f8a8-4869-b16b-f4f3934f28ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52457ed3-f58a-4047-bf83-72a36d477308",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The questions you were asking are like categories (like \"topping\" or \"quantity\"), and the answers (like \"mushroom\" or \"2\") are the specific values. In programming, those categories become parameters, and the specific values are called arguments.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52457ed3-f58a-4047-bf83-72a36d477308",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e22fdb07-ccdc-4a99-9e57-5aac5d747063",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define parameter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define argument."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e22fdb07-ccdc-4a99-9e57-5aac5d747063",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea506632-3276-4e31-9179-9be8f4c7e161",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Students will complete Lesson 6, Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Explore a shopping app that doesn’t use a function with parameters and use their Unit Guide to note what inputs are needed.\n- Level 2: Plan and use AI to generate a function that updates the page based on an input (parameter)\n- Level 3: Using the AI-generated function as a model, create an additional parameterized function that updates the page",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Why Functions Matter",
        "progression_name": "Exploring Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea506632-3276-4e31-9179-9be8f4c7e161",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6b734bf-774d-488e-bc75-d0ff39193a47",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use a table to identify actions, inputs (parameters), and outputs and connect them to how a function is defined, used, and called in a program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What user actions, inputs, and output might a shopping app have?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the ways in which the user interacts with the shopping app such as selecting products and adding them to the cart. They recognize that there are many possible inputs for a function, such as size, color, and price. Finally, they suggest output that includes those inputs. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to show possible user actions, inputs for a function, and a possible output. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6b734bf-774d-488e-bc75-d0ff39193a47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46c3fb9c-ab93-4728-ac7e-27fae467b2c1",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slides to show how an example of how to define a function, use a function’s parameters, and call the function in the program.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect each step back to the Guess the Pizza activity. Emphasize that details like topping and quantity become parameters (inputs), and when the function is called, the specific values (like 3 or \"mushroom\") are the arguments. The function uses these inputs to update what appears on the screen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46c3fb9c-ab93-4728-ac7e-27fae467b2c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd3f6584-a9a8-47a7-a8ef-e97347b30ed9",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd3f6584-a9a8-47a7-a8ef-e97347b30ed9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf92794f-bd7e-4826-9d1e-84efe142a25d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide and explain that quantity and topping are parameters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide and explain the purpose of the `quantity` parameter. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the parameters are used in the function to create the output. Point out which parts come from the inputs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the parameters are used in the function to create the output. Point out which parts stay the same.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the function is called when the button is clicked, and point out how the values passed in become the inputs for the function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide and explain the purpose of the `topping` parameter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the function is called when the button is clicked and point out how the arguments are connected to the parameters from the function definition.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out how the values passed in become the inputs for the function.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that the values passed in (like 3 and \"mushroom\") are the arguments, which are the specific inputs used when the function runs, rather than the general categories (parameters)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf92794f-bd7e-4826-9d1e-84efe142a25d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78484e1e-bad2-4334-8c37-fede5c2af71d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78484e1e-bad2-4334-8c37-fede5c2af71d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "079e10dc-d250-4b7c-8f6a-b3ac542f1674",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4–6. \n- Level 4: Students will first plan a function by identifying which user interactions should change the output. \n- Level 5: Students should use their Sketch as context in AI Tutor and build a function that changes what is shown on the page based on different inputs. \n- Level 6: Student explain what their function does, what input it takes, and how that input changes the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Support students as they identify inputs, generate their function, and test different values. \n\nAsk:\n- “What inputs does your function take?”\n- “How do those inputs change the result on the page?”\n- “Where are the inputs used in the function?”\n- “Where is the function called?”",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Writing Functions with Parameters",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to start, give them a frame such as: “This function updates ______ when given ______.” Encourage students to compare their Sketch to their code and test different inputs to make sure the output changes as expected."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "079e10dc-d250-4b7c-8f6a-b3ac542f1674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2de9d97-da66-472d-9a1d-3e8286ca13e4",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2de9d97-da66-472d-9a1d-3e8286ca13e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40beb2c2-16b8-4caf-827a-6b09e1a97dcf",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts:\nHow did parameters make your function more flexible?\nWhere did AI help or confuse you in the process?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Facilitate a brief discussion about working with parameters. Prompt students to share specific examples of how their functions that they created were made more reusable and flexible by adding one or more parameters. Then ask students to share their experiences using the AI tool for coding assistance. Encourage them to discuss both the benefits (e.g., faster code generation, debugging hints) and the drawbacks (e.g., potential for incorrect suggestions, difficulty understanding the generated code). ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Normalize the idea that AI is a tool and not always perfect. Emphasize the importance of critical thinking and verifying AI-generated output. Discuss how AI can be a powerful learning aid when used effectively."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40beb2c2-16b8-4caf-827a-6b09e1a97dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "786a14de-22e2-4674-8ba6-edbddcad11cb",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "786a14de-22e2-4674-8ba6-edbddcad11cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
