      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how interactive elements on a webpage support usability using the POUR principles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how the POUR principles apply to interactivity on a web page."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c734957-d8ea-4c33-adf9-ed660b663be5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What is changing on the page?*\n- *When does it change?*\n- *Which part of POUR does it support? How?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify what changes on a dynamic page, when it changes, and connect those changes to POUR:\n- The password feedback messages (errors and “strong password”) and their colors update.\n- As the user types in the password field (in real time).\n- Perceivable → Users can see clear visual feedback (colors/messages); Understandable → Messages explain what is wrong or correct.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c734957-d8ea-4c33-adf9-ed660b663be5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "871ca166-b281-4239-9e07-8d7cd2702c7b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "871ca166-b281-4239-9e07-8d7cd2702c7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fae7a42-1b39-404d-a182-988d9b11100c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify how interactive features change a page and begin organizing that behavior using functions. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 1, Level 1. Students explore different dynamic, interactive features and see how they change a page. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, guide students to notice:the event (click, input, etc.), the code that runs when the event happens, and the specific lines that update the page. If students are stuck, prompt: \"Find the line that changes something on the page. What is it doing?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore Dynamic Features",
        "progression_name": "Explore Dynamic Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fae7a42-1b39-404d-a182-988d9b11100c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc151019-70c9-4a93-8dd5-6dca9c6b74dd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the core components of a dynamic web page (Event, State, Logic, Update) and ensure students understand what each one means.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example and have students identify each part (Event, State, Logic, Update). Ask them to connect each part to what is happening in the app.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc151019-70c9-4a93-8dd5-6dca9c6b74dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24a1a559-2518-4e2e-a735-fc62a762ab0a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students plan, build, and evaluate a dynamic feature using functions, logic, and user interaction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 2 through Level 7.\n- **Level 2:** Brainstorm a dynamic feature with AI Chat.\n- **Level 3:** Prompt AI to generate a User Interface (HTML and CSS)\n- **Level 4:** Use the plan to prompt AI to generate the dynamic feature\n- **Level 5:** Identify and label parts of an interaction (element, event listener, event handler)\n- **Level 6:** Trace and explain what happens inside the event handler\n- **Level 7:** Evaluate the feature using POUR and identify improvements\n- **Level 8:** Generate and test a dynamic feature using a plan",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Plan and Generate Dynamic Features",
        "progression_name": "Generate Dynamic Features",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Push students to move beyond “it works” to understanding how it works. As they build, have them pause and point to:\n- where the interaction starts (event),\n- what information is being tracked (variables/state), and\n- which lines actually update the page.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 4-8 to assess whether students can apply variables and parameters to make dynamic changes to the DOM and revise interactions to improve usability. Look for evidence that students use variables to control what updates on the page, pass values through functions or parameters, and make intentional changes that improve clarity and user experience (e.g., clearer feedback, more visible updates). Students should be able to explain how their interaction works and how it supports the user.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24a1a559-2518-4e2e-a735-fc62a762ab0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "897cb38f-da38-4924-abfe-2a1f1c2ab8a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to follow the paths on the flowchart that apply to them and complete the corresponding task. Emphasize that they should be ready to explain the path they took.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What task did you complete? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice tracing nested decisions step-by-step, recognizing how each condition leads to a specific outcome. This builds understanding that nested conditionals evaluate one condition inside another to reach a final result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When might a web app need to make different decisions for different users?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**Students understand that real-world apps use nested conditionals to personalize experiences and handle different user paths. For example:\n- **Age gating:** Showing different content if a user is under 13 or over 18\n- **Login status:** Showing a dashboard if you’re logged in, or a sign-up page if you’re not\n- **Recommendations:** Suggesting videos, products, or music based on past behavior\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
        "lesson_activity.key": "456fe5bb-2199-4edb-9127-15232f34397b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build foundational understanding of nested if/else statements by translating a simple flowchart into working code. They’ll learn how each decision branch connects to a UI change, define what nested conditionals are, and use AI support to debug and confirm that all paths behave as expected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define nested conditionals. Emphasize that a nested conditional is just a conditional inside another conditional, where one question leads to another question.\n\n\n\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Flowchart to Code",
        "progression_name": "Movie Spoiler App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbcb3680-61a2-4bce-8a52-9589414ab545",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just looked at a nested conditional in the warm up. If one condition is true, you continue down the path to the next condition. In this example, we first ask if a student is wearing sneakers. Then, depending on their answer, we ask a new question like “Wearing glasses?” or “Wearing blue?” Similarly, AI uses a branching, tree-like structure to model decisions and their possible consequences. So when we build nested conditionals in code, we’re actually modeling the same kind of thinking AI systems use to classify things.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students trace through each path individually. See if they can come up with additional questions to ask that could lead to new outcomes. For example, instead of “Wearing blue?” you could ask “Wearing boots?”."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbcb3680-61a2-4bce-8a52-9589414ab545",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95405c81-2f98-4e9a-85d4-6e47fe14b436",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 1 and their Unit Guide flowchart.\n- Level 1: Students will explore the code and complete their Unit Guide.\n\n <i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students test out the app’s if/else logic, have them trace through the code to see what they understand. Ask them to explain how each path changes the UI and how they’re using all four test cases (True/True, True/ False, False/True, False/False) to verify correctness.\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Nested Conditionals",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Highlight the roles of a Front-End Developer (implementing the code) and a QA Engineer (executing tests and logging bugs) in the software development process."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95405c81-2f98-4e9a-85d4-6e47fe14b436",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61212c29-1817-495b-a554-fd4d3a9c1e47",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why does the app need to check both conditions instead of just one?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that having multiple conditions can affect the program’s outcome. Checking each condition adds new possible paths (combinations of true/false), in this case leading to four different outcomes. Whereas checking only one condition would lead to incorrect or incomplete behavior."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61212c29-1817-495b-a554-fd4d3a9c1e47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "384618b4-848c-490e-b2e0-000ab4cbf63b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "384618b4-848c-490e-b2e0-000ab4cbf63b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8a81b15-9b2b-4110-b25a-dd04155c20e3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that nested conditionals build on what they learned about conditionals in Lesson 5.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the conditionals code and give students time to read it. Click through the animations to remind students that this is a multi-step process: initialize any variables, define the if condition, and define the else condition.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animations to show students how to represent a nested conditional flowchart in code. Have them fill in the blanks in their Unit Guide as you show the code.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Introduction to Nested Conditionals"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8a81b15-9b2b-4110-b25a-dd04155c20e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of nested if/else logic by planning, coding, and testing a multi-decision feature. They’ll use AI to turn their flowchart into working code, debug and refine AI’s logic, and verify that all four paths behave as intended through systematic testing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 and 3 by planning nested decisions and finally implement them in code. They will use scaffolds like the Test Cases table in their activity guide and AI support to refine their work.\n- Level 2: Students will plan a feature by creating a flowchart with nested decisions and test cases.\n- Level 3: Students will implement their plan in code, debug using test cases, and refine with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students notice how AI can make implementing nested conditionals faster and easier but if their logic is confusing, the AI’s output may be wrong. As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart and ensure they are testing all four outcomes.",
        "name": "Plan, Code, and Test (20 mins)",
        "progression_name": "Plan App Logic"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "122baadc-4e1f-43dd-b058-1f2521771223",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build & Test Your Logic"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "122baadc-4e1f-43dd-b058-1f2521771223",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80003ce6-9db6-41b3-8ce6-c841704e97c9",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80003ce6-9db6-41b3-8ce6-c841704e97c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cc903e6-d289-4af0-b075-b43a7200f044",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the discussion questions:\n- How did you check AI’s work? \n- Did AI miss any paths in your flowchart?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should discuss that they tested all four possible outcomes and matched what they saw on screen to their flowchart. Students may notice that the AI assumed one condition mattered more (e.g., finished movie = true) — a small example of bias in logic or oversimplification.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt students to connect this debugging step to real-world examples of biased or incomplete algorithms (e.g., recommendation systems showing only one kind of result)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cc903e6-d289-4af0-b075-b43a7200f044",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Review the Question of the Day. Have a couple students project their flowchart on the board and briefly trace through branches for a specific input/output scenario. Then have them show where that UI outcome appears in their code. Optional: Have students answer “A test case that initially broke my code was ___ . I debugged it by ___”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
        "lesson_activity.key": "141bc852-7894-47e3-be59-bbca6ebdf62a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the two example \"style rules\" on the slide. Have students come up with their own rule, using either 'and' or 'or' and discuss it with a partner. Emphasize that these should be school-appropriate, positive descriptors only. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to explore how combining conditional statements using AND and OR can make our programs more robust. Real web apps often need to check more than one condition at a time. Simple if-else statements can only handle one test, but combining conditions helps your code make smarter decisions — like showing confidential information only if a user is logged in AND verified.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will be able to explore web pages that use combined conditional statements with && (AND) and || (OR).\n\n</i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1 and 2. Have students work in pairs to predict what message will appear for each combination of checkboxes before testing. Then have them toggle the checkboxes to test their predictions and record patterns they notice in their activity guide. Avoid explaining how AND and OR work at this stage—focus on observation and pattern-finding.\n- Level 1: Students test how different checkbox combinations affect the result and record what they notice for all four cases: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 2: Students repeat the process with a different rule and compare how the results change across the same four cases.\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Conditions",
        "progression_name": "Using AND and OR",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to make predictions before testing. If needed, model how to toggle the checkboxes, but do not explain how the logic works yet. If students ask for the “rule,” redirect with: \"What are you noticing so far?\" or \"Try another combination and see what happens.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What patterns did you notice in Levels 1 and 2 when the result turned true or false?*\n- *How were the results in Level 2 different from Level 1?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize patterns in how results change across combinations of inputs and begin comparing those patterns between the two levels. Look for students to notice that in one case the result is only true when both conditions are true, while in the other case the result is true when at least one condition is true. Students may describe this in their own words (e.g., “both have to be on” vs “just one works”), which sets up the transition to formal terminology.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6b7cb2d-4f01-4d94-858a-f4a0186831ab",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve been testing different combinations and noticing patterns in when the result is true or false. These patterns actually have names in programming. When a result is only true if both conditions are true, we call that AND. When a result is true if at least one condition is true, we call that OR.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6b7cb2d-4f01-4d94-858a-f4a0186831ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7af2c8c-2fe7-4aa0-8cf7-c4bb9ef54a65",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "</i> **Do This:** Define AND (&&) by building on the patterns students just discussed. Ask students to restate when the result was true in Level 1, and guide them to recognize that both conditions needed to be true. Name this pattern as AND and connect it to the table, pointing out that only the case where both values are true results in a true outcome. Also clarify that AND combines two conditions (like the two checkboxes) to produce one result.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students describe the pattern informally (e.g., “both have to be on”), affirm their language and then introduce the term AND (&&) as the formal name for that idea."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7af2c8c-2fe7-4aa0-8cf7-c4bb9ef54a65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94b34d96-c672-40d3-99f7-2e6508efbed4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "</i> **Do This:** Define OR (||) by building on the patterns students identified in Level 2. Ask students to describe when the result was true, and guide them to recognize that the result was true when at least one condition was true. Name this pattern as OR and connect it to the table, pointing out that all cases except when both values are false result in a true outcome. Also clarify that OR combines two conditions (like the two checkboxes) to produce one result.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students describe the pattern informally (e.g., “only one has to be on”), affirm their language and then introduce the term OR (||) as the formal name for that idea."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94b34d96-c672-40d3-99f7-2e6508efbed4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f2c5c68-725a-44ca-b6a2-9b1853522e47",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "</i> **Do This:** Define truth table by connecting it directly to the tables students just completed. Ask students to look back at their work and recognize that they already recorded all possible combinations and outcomes. Introduce “truth table” as the formal name for that structure. Have students complete the truth tables in their unit guide using the patterns they observed in the activity. You may choose to complete the tables with the students or complete the first row together as a class, then have students finish the remaining rows independently or with a partner.\n\n</i> **Do This:** Explain that each row in the table becomes one path in the flowchart. Walk through one case step-by-step (e.g., both conditions are true), pointing to the flowchart as you trace the path to an outcome. Then briefly show how the other cases would follow different paths. Make sure to explain that when ‘Has Ticket’ is false, we don’t need to check the second condition when working with AND—both remaining cases (False/True and False/False) lead to No Access.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, pause after modeling one path and ask: “Where would a different case go?” or “Which row from the table does this path represent?” Have students point or describe before you confirm."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f2c5c68-725a-44ca-b6a2-9b1853522e47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8acbaa1-7e7f-4a4c-95dd-6e0a29ca8656",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "</i> **Do This:** Walk students through the code snippet. This code should be familiar to them as it comes from Level 1. Click the animation to highlight where the `access` variable checks `hasTicket` && `hasPermission` values.\n\n</i> **Do This:** Walk students through the code snippet. This code should be familiar to them as it comes from Level 2. Click the animation to highlight where the `canPay` variable checks `hasCash` && `hasCoupon` values."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8acbaa1-7e7f-4a4c-95dd-6e0a29ca8656",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "796724f3-9eb9-4deb-bc6a-05430a44b96f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "796724f3-9eb9-4deb-bc6a-05430a44b96f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use a truth table to design a flowchart that represents all possible outcomes of two conditions, then translate that logic into code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 3 and 4. \n- Level 3: Students fill in a truth table and create a flowchart for their chosen context in Sketch Lab. \n- Level 4: Students convert their flowchart into code and test it for all 4 possible outcomes.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Coding a Multi-Condition Flowchart ",
        "progression_name": "Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to check that their flowchart includes all four possible cases. If students get stuck, prompt them to refer back to their tables and ask: “Which case is this representing?”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c65051fd-cd04-476d-a930-f82677f6e5ea",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Code Your Rule",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 3 and 4 to assess whether students can accurately represent and apply multi-condition logic. Look for evidence that students:\n- Create a truth table that includes all four possible combinations of inputs\n- Design a flowchart where each outcome matches a row from their truth table\n- Ensure their flowchart includes all four possible cases\n- Translate their flowchart into code that produces the correct result for each case\n\nIf students are missing outcomes or have incorrect results, prompt them to revisit their truth table and identify which case is not yet represented.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c65051fd-cd04-476d-a930-f82677f6e5ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how an app can use multiple conditions together to make decisions, using examples from the lesson (such as checkboxes, AND/OR logic, or flowcharts). After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. As students share, highlight responses that mention combining conditions and checking multiple possibilities (e.g., using AND or OR) to determine an outcome.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1df1a9ab-dab4-41e4-8499-f8e04a7b1ea0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "304dbb6e-36a8-4030-98e6-9cab0f72f600",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** You apply for a student discount but the AI says \"Not eligible\". Why might this happen? What rule might the AI be using?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is for students to begin recognizing that AI decisions are based on underlying rules or conditions rather than independent reasoning. Students should start to infer that these rules often rely on thresholds (such as income limits) or required information, and that unclear or incomplete rules can lead to outcomes that feel confusing or unfair. Listen for students suggesting possible conditions the AI might be using and beginning to connect decisions to specific criteria rather than vague ideas.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to move beyond surface-level responses, prompt them to think about what information the AI might need to make a decision and what could be missing or misinterpreted. You can introduce a simple example like an income cutoff to help ground their thinking, but avoid fully defining the rule for them so they continue to generate their own ideas.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "304dbb6e-36a8-4030-98e6-9cab0f72f600",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "881b123e-c5ec-4e37-a8af-20a874479bbf",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The AI isn’t deciding this on its own — it’s following a rule. Today, we’re going to learn how to design and understand those rules.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "881b123e-c5ec-4e37-a8af-20a874479bbf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e341dda-5e82-4111-81e4-72714a880d12",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e341dda-5e82-4111-81e4-72714a880d12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that a value changes in response to user actions and controls what appears on the screen. This sets up the NOT operator as a way to represent that change and prepares students to read more complex conditions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1. Have them click the toggle multiple times and observe what changes, paying attention to both the visual changes and the value on the screen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students notice both the visual changes and the isOn value. Some may focus only on the appearance and miss the value changing. Prompt them to be specific by asking what exactly is changing each time they click. Encourage students to click multiple times to see the pattern.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The NOT Operator",
        "progression_name": "Boolean and UI Toggles Panel"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide the display the discussion prompts.\n- *What changed each time you clicked? What stayed the same?*\n- *What value was changing?*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is for students to clearly articulate that a value in the program is changing each time they click, and that this change controls what they see on the screen. Students should move beyond general observations like “it switches” and instead identify that the isOn value changes from true to false and drives the visual updates. Listen for students connecting the visible changes to this underlying value.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give vague responses, ask follow-up questions like “What does the value say before and after you click?” or “What exactly is changing in the program?” to push them toward more precise explanations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53c68dea-e8b4-4da8-ac33-ef2517bf252e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So we’ve seen that this value flips each time you click — from false to true and back again. In code, we have a way to represent that exact change. It’s called the NOT operator.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53c68dea-e8b4-4da8-ac33-ef2517bf252e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bd5637e-dc9e-4692-9623-4ccde69a7b6d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define NOT (!). Connect directly to the previous discussion by naming what students observed. Explain that when they clicked the toggle, the value changed from false to true and back again, and that in code, this change is represented using the NOT (!) operator. Emphasize that NOT describes the behavior they just observed—flipping a value from true to false or false to true.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the explanation concise and focused on the idea of “flipping a value.” Avoid introducing additional terminology or over-explaining the definition. Let the visual table reinforce the pattern and use student observations from the previous discussion to anchor understanding.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bd5637e-dc9e-4692-9623-4ccde69a7b6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "037cf347-ca20-454c-bc5d-500294221327",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students first predict what !isOn will be in each case. After a brief think time, direct them to record their predictions in the “Fill in the Boolean” section of their unit guide. Then walk through the example and explicitly point out how the NOT (!) operator is used in front of a value. Show that adding ! changes the value from true to false or false to true. Connect this directly to the idea that NOT flips the value, and now we are seeing how that behavior is written in code.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- Have students commit to a prediction before writing to avoid copying from peers or waiting for the answer. The goal is for students to think about how the value changes, not just fill in the blanks.\n- Focus on helping students see that ! is placed directly before a value to change it. Avoid introducing additional terminology or rules here—this moment is about recognizing how the concept of “flipping a value” appears in code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "037cf347-ca20-454c-bc5d-500294221327",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "462979db-a2bc-491e-a09b-eb7b7f052e09",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the condition and give students time to think before discussing. Ask them to identify all the different ways the condition could be true. Guide them to break the expression into parts, noticing that some values must work together (like `isStudent` && `hasID`), while others can make the condition true on their own (like `hasSpecialPass`). After discussing, have students annotate the condition in their Unit Guide to capture their thinking. Emphasize reasoning through the structure rather than trying to read the entire condition at once.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them to focus on one part of the condition at a time and describe what would happen if only that part were true. Avoid introducing formal rules or terminology about order of operations here—keep the focus on reasoning and interpretation.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "462979db-a2bc-491e-a09b-eb7b7f052e09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc71bf5f-950a-4c7c-b604-8a56fad78e58",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Where have you seen something switch ON/OFF?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is for students to connect the idea of a value switching between two states to familiar examples in everyday technology. Students should recognize that many apps use this kind of logic, even if they don’t see the code behind it. Listen for students identifying examples like mute/unmute, password visibility, or dark mode, and help them connect those examples back to the idea of a value flipping between true and false.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only list examples without making the connection, prompt them to explain what is actually changing behind the scenes. Ask questions like “What is the app tracking?” or “What would the true/false value be in this situation?” to help them link real-world examples to Boolean thinking."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc71bf5f-950a-4c7c-b604-8a56fad78e58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students move from writing logic to understanding and evaluating it. They analyze existing code, use AI to explain and critique logic, refactor nested conditionals into combined Boolean expressions, and consider how rules impact different users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2 through 4. Explain that they will act as developers reviewing and improving existing logic, rather than writing everything from scratch.\n- **Level 2:** Students step into the role of IT Support to investigate and fix a logic issue.\n- **Level 3:** Students analyze provided code, use AI to explain and evaluate the logic, and refactor it into a combined Boolean expression.\n- **Level 4:** Students evaluate when the rule is true, who might be affected by it, and how it could be improved for fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are actually reading and reasoning about the code before using AI. Some students may rely too quickly on AI explanations without verifying them. Prompt students to explain the logic in their own words and check whether the AI explanation matches what the code is doing. As students refactor, check that they are preserving the original logic while rewriting it using !, &&, and ||. In Level 4, listen for whether students can clearly describe when the rule is true and who might be affected by it.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Work with ! and Complex Conditions",
        "progression_name": "Work with ! and Complex Conditions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to treat AI as a thinking partner rather than a solution generator. Have them ask AI to explain the logic, then compare that explanation to their own understanding before making changes. Remind students that their goal is not just to get working code, but to understand how the logic works and how it can be improved.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 2, 3 and 4 to assess whether students can interpret and refactor complex conditions using !, &&, and ||, and explain when a rule is true and how it may impact different users.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how they can design and debug more complex rules by breaking conditions into parts, testing different cases, and checking how the logic behaves. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. As students share, highlight responses that mention breaking down conditions, checking when a rule is true, and evaluating how logic affects different users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988d6b5c-9198-4a2e-99f7-224be3e8f64e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s an example of an app, game, or website that reacts in a way you really like ? Why do you think it feels satisfying or fun?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify qualities of engaging interactivity to inspire their own app designs and ground today’s planning in real user experiences.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Project Rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Reactive Logic Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their magazine concept to showcasing their final product.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work through Levels 1 - 3 to begin Milestone 1: Plan Your Interactive App. Students will move through a sequence of levels to define their app idea, design their layout, and map how their app works.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore each level, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “What is the main interaction in your app?”\n- “Where does the user interact with your app, and what happens in response?”\n- “How does your flowchart represent what your app should do?”\n\nUse these check-ins to ensure students are connecting their idea, layout, and logic.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to move quickly from idea to design to logic. These levels are meant to help them get a clear starting point—not to fully plan every detail."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 4 to complete their reflection on Milestone 1. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to discuss and share these reflection questions with a thought partner. \n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one part of your app that you’re most excited to see working? What’s one piece you’re not sure how to build yet?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate clear goals and challenges for their first build session, helping them prioritize tasks and seek targeted support.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 5 to begin building their app. This is the point where their ideas become interactive. Encourage students to build one working interaction first before adding more complexity. Emphasize that strong apps are built in small, testable pieces. If students feel unsure where to start, remind them to focus on the first clear cause-and-effect moment from their plan.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a working first interaction. Ask check-in questions like:\n- “What is the first interaction you’re building?”\n- “What should happen when the user interacts with your app?”\n- “How will you know your interaction is working correctly?”\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck — encourage them to use AI Tutor to help debug and build before stepping in directly.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 6 to complete their reflection on Milestone 2. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students respond and discuss, listen for reflections that identify concrete next steps. Encourage students to document these next steps clearly so they can pick up with them in the next class period.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one key challenge or problem they plan to solve. Highlight that thoughtful reflection is a sign of growth—it shows they're thinking like real designers preparing to polish their final product.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one bug, glitch, or weird behavior your app is showing right now?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice describing and analyzing problems out loud, normalizing debugging as part of the creative process and preparing for peer or AI-assisted troubleshooting.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Peer Feedback with TAG handout.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c42355bb-a76a-4416-b3c2-fb1dd6dbd674",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up and exchange apps. Students should review one to two peers’ projects and use the TAG method (Tell, Ask, Give) to provide feedback using the provided handout. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it’s an opportunity to gather feedback and identify ways to improve their app.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c42355bb-a76a-4416-b3c2-fb1dd6dbd674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a89421e0-7ea0-4bbb-a5b7-1939119e1b51",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 7 and 8 to complete and test the app’s interactivity and then get feedback from a partner. \n\nStudents will:\n- finish implementing their app’s logic\n- test how it responds to different inputs\n- get feedback from a peer\n\nDirect students to continue working in WebLab (Level 7) to complete their app’s logic, then move to Level 8 to participate in peer feedback using the TAG method.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “Are all your planned conditions working as expected?”\n- “What happens when you try different inputs?”\n- “How are you using functions and variables to control your app’s behavior?”\n\nUse these check-ins to ensure students are testing their app and refining how it works.\n",
        "name": "Milestone 3",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a89421e0-7ea0-4bbb-a5b7-1939119e1b51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 9 to complete their reflection on Milestone 3. \n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share how they’ve been testing their app or what they plan to test next. Emphasize that repeated testing and refinement mirrors what real software engineers do—continually running, observing, and adjusting their programs to make them stronger and more reliable. This helps students see debugging and iteration as essential parts of the professional development process, not just final clean-up.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone were testing your app right now, what’s the one thing you’d want them to notice first?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the purpose and user experience of their app, setting an intentional focus for peer testing and feedback during today’s class.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d83eee5e-016c-422f-9296-190fd90cfa4a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to go to Lesson 11, Level 10 to refine their app by improving functionality, design, and code using feedback. \n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d83eee5e-016c-422f-9296-190fd90cfa4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f3f7ce1-81b6-4b38-ac02-182b891c521b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f3f7ce1-81b6-4b38-ac02-182b891c521b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e96f7f4-1e89-4dda-9eb7-ea2faeb0539b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e96f7f4-1e89-4dda-9eb7-ea2faeb0539b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit their Milestone 3 reflection on Level 9 add on to what they wrote the previous class period.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students revisit their reflections today, point out that adding new insights after another round of testing is exactly how professional engineers document progress. Encourage them to notice what changed, what improved, and what new questions emerged—this shows they’re thinking like real developers who learn from every test cycle to make smarter, more informed decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one thing you still need to check or polish before your app feels ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students self-assess their progress and identify final next steps, using available resources and the rubric to guide productive final work time.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the Project Rubric. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 10 to finish developing their Reactive Logic projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4 – Finalize. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Reactive Logic project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Reactive Logic Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've gone from planning and designing to building, testing, and refining. This reflection is your chance to capture how you've grown — not just as a coder or designer, but as someone who collaborates with AI intentionally.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8ee2d5c-5b77-476e-85b0-4ee7153d545e",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 11 to complete the Final Project Reflection. Explain that this final step helps them consolidate their learning and ensure their site meets key design goals — usability, consistency, accessibility, and thoughtful use of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to elaborate on their answers with specific examples of how they used feedback or iteration to improve their project. If students finish early, prompt them to help peers finalize their submissions or review their rubric one more time for completeness.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "You can use the Reactive Logic Planning Guide as evidence for the Algorithmic Planning rubric criteria. The Final Reflection can be used as evidence for the Process and Reflection rubric criteria.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8ee2d5c-5b77-476e-85b0-4ee7153d545e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e3ecb35-db6d-4a65-ae41-e03690cd3598",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e3ecb35-db6d-4a65-ae41-e03690cd3598",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d6aee7a-1a7a-4be9-98fe-08347e301657"
      }
    }
  ],
